<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<StyleSet xmlns="http://adamantium/ui"
          xmlns:x="http://adamantium/ui/xaml/extensions">

    <!-- The knob both sliders take hold of, as ONE style rather than as an inline template in each of them - because it
         now has a STATE (it glows while it is being dragged), and a state needs a trigger, and a trigger needs a part it
         can name. An inline Thumb.Template has no name to offer, and duplicating this into the two slider sets would put
         two styles on one selector, which is its own bug.

         Opted into by class - <Thumb Classes="SliderKnob"/> - so the scrollbar's thumb and the splitter's are untouched.

         WHITE IN BOTH APPEARANCES, like the switch's: it is a physical knob, and a knob does not change colour with the
         lighting. What changes with the appearance is what SEPARATES it from the background, and that is a colour the
         variant supplies (see SliderKnobShadowColor - a real shadow in the light appearance, nothing in the dark one,
         where a white circle on #323232 already stands out).

         HELD = LIT. An aura, not a bigger shadow: a shadow says how high a thing floats, and the knob does not rise
         when you grab it - it becomes the thing you are working with, which is what light says. It appears only while
         dragging, so the resting control never glows.

         The 4 of reach is not a taste either: the knob is 20 in a 28 control, so there are exactly four clear on each
         side, and neither band may ask for more - the engine does not grow layout for a halo, and the excess would be
         cut by the first ancestor that clips with nothing to say it had happened. -->

    <Style Selector="Thumb.SliderKnob">
        <Setter Property="Template">
            <Setter.Value>
                <ControlTemplate TargetType="Thumb">
                    <Border x:Name="Knob"
                            CornerRadius="10"
                            Background="#FFFFFF"
                            BorderBrush="{ObservableResource SliderKnobRimColor}"
                            BorderThickness="1">
                        <Border.Shadow>
                            <!-- Opacity 1: the strength lives in the palette colour's alpha, so a variant that wants no
                                 shadow says so in one place instead of in two that have to agree. -->
                            <Shadow OffsetY="1" BlurRadius="3" Opacity="1"
                                    Color="{ObservableResource SliderKnobShadowColor}"/>
                        </Border.Shadow>
                        <!-- DECLARED HERE, SWITCHED OFF - not handed over by the trigger as a finished object. An Aura
                             built inside Setter.Value only comes into existence at the moment of the drag, which is why
                             it arrived late. Living in the template it is there from the start, and the trigger below
                             has nothing to do but flip the switch - which is exactly what Aura.IsEnabled is for.

                             AND THE COLOUR IS {ObservableResource}, NOT {ResourceReference}. A ResourceReference is a
                             one-shot lookup performed WHEN THE ELEMENT ATTACHES to the tree; an Aura is a property
                             VALUE and never attaches, so the lookup never ran and it kept Aura's own default - white.
                             Every other colour-served key in the themes (the tab strip's edge fades, the shimmer
                             stops - all of them on GradientStop.Color, which sits exactly where this does) uses
                             ObservableResource for the same reason. -->
                        <Border.Aura>
                            <!-- A RIM plus a short fade, not a wide soft glow: Spread is the solid part the fade starts
                                 outside of. Four pixels of soft haze around a bright white circle is swallowed by the
                                 knob and reads as nothing having happened. Total reach stays 4 - all the room there
                                 is - and is spent on something that can be seen. -->
                            <Aura x:Name="KnobAura" IsEnabled="False"
                                  Spread="1" Radius="3" Opacity="0.95"
                                  Color="{ObservableResource SliderKnobAuraColor}"/>
                        </Border.Aura>
                    </Border>
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <Style Selector="Thumb.SliderKnob">
        <!-- IsDragging is set in Thumb.BeginDrag, which runs from the mouse-DOWN handler - so this is a press, not a
             movement, which is what a Mac answers to. -->
        <PropertyTrigger Property="IsDragging" Value="true">
            <!-- The knob's own fill, FIRST, because it is the half of this that always reads. The aura is the accent,
                 and a knob spends half its travel standing on the accent-filled part of the rail, where an accent glow
                 is its background's colour to the byte - invisible by construction, and the reason the glow alone
                 looked like it fired only some of the time. What is behind the knob cannot hide the knob. -->
            <Setter TargetName="Knob" Property="Background" Value="{ObservableResource SliderKnobFillPressed}"/>
            <!-- The knob's own rim goes accent too, and not for decoration: the aura's inner edge and the knob's fill
                 edge are the SAME contour, both anti-aliased, so neither covers the pixel they share - and whatever
                 lies behind shows through it as a hairline. On a slider what lies behind is the rail, which is exactly
                 where it was seen. The rim is drawn WITH the knob, so it covers that seam. -->
            <Setter TargetName="Knob" Property="BorderBrush" Value="{ThemeResource AccentFillColorDefault}"/>
            <Setter TargetName="KnobAura" Property="IsEnabled" Value="True"/>
        </PropertyTrigger>
    </Style>

</StyleSet>
